START-HERE -- a dozen things to type, and how to get back out==============================================================The disk is alphabetical and long.  This is a short list of programs thatrun with nothing set up: no arguments, no data files to find, no terminaltype to get right.  It exists so that "does this thing work?" has an answeryou can reach in one line.`about <name>' will tell you what any of them is, where it came from, whatit reads and whether its source is here.PRINTS SOMETHING AND STOPS--------------------------  fortune        a quotation  cookie         another one, from a different collection  calendar       what happened on today's date  ascii          the character set, four ways  bush           a tree, drawn  banner HELLO   large lettersRUNS UNTIL YOU STOP IT----------------------  rain           rain on the screen  worms          worms across the screen  life           Conway's lifeThese hold the terminal.  Ctrl-C ends all three.TAKES A WHILE-------------  advent         Colossal Cave.  Type WEST to begin.  hack           the dungeon.  `?' for keys, `Q' then `y' to quit.  larn           another dungeon.  Ctrl-C to quit.  bog            Boggle.  Ctrl-C to quit.  backgammon     it explains itself if you ask for instructionsGETTING OUT-----------Ctrl-C ends nearly everything here.  The ones that want something else sayso, above.  If a program has taken the terminal and Ctrl-C does not reachit, Ctrl-E then Ctrl-C is os9exec's own interrupt.WHERE TO GO NEXT----------------  DOC/CATEGORIES     everything, grouped by what it is for  DOC/INDEX          everything, alphabetical, one line each  DOC/README-RUNNING three ways to run this disk  about <name>       one program, in fullIf you want to keep one on your own system rather than run it from here,`keep <name>' copies the program and the files it reads.  DOC/README-KEEPexplains what it records and how `drop' undoes it.